  "title": "CHAPTER 2: GEOGRAPHY OF HIMACHAL PRADESH",
  "order": 2,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "tfdb0fx",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2.1 Location and Extent"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The state's geography is divided into three main zones: Outer Himalayas (Shiwaliks), Inner Himalayas, and Greater Himalayas.",
        "Five major perennial rivers drain Himachal Pradesh: Satluj, Beas, Ravi, Chenab, and Yamuna.",
        "Shilla Peak in Kinnaur district stands as the highest elevation point in the state at 6,816 meters.",
        "High-altitude mountain passes (Darras/Jots) serve as crucial strategic routes connecting remote valleys like Lahaul, Spiti, and Kullu.",
        "Bara Shigri is the largest glacier in Himachal Pradesh, feeding the Chandra river in Lahaul-Spiti."
      ],
      "id": "par36xj"
    },
